>> title: Ohitashi
>> category: Vegetables & Sides
>> tags: japanese, spinach, dashi, blanched, side, quick, vegetarian
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: おひたし, ohitashi, ohitashi, お浸し, 御浸し, ほうれん草のおひたし, horenso no ohitashi, blanched spinach in dashi, dressed greens
>> pairs-with: dashi, mentsuyu, nikujaga
>> servings: 4
>> time: 45 min
>> slack: narrow — spinach past about forty seconds in the water is a wet rope and no amount of squeezing firms it back up; the steep at the other end is the forgiving half and will go overnight without complaint
>> step.1: Two ingredients and a technique. The seasoning is 浸し地 — dashi 8 : soy 1 : mirin 1 — and it is a bath, not a dressing: the greens sit in it rather than wearing it.
>> step.2: blanch 30 sec, then ice
>> step.3: squeeze and cut
>> step.4: steep 30 min
>> step.5: squeeze once more and top

Two ingredients and a technique. The seasoning is 浸し地 — dashi 8 : soy 1 : mirin 1 — and it is a bath rather than a dressing: the greens sit in it and take it up, instead of wearing it on the outside.

Blanch @spinach{1%lb}(450 g; bought on the bunch, roots trimmed but the bunch kept whole) in boiling @salted water{16%cups}(3.8 L; with 1 Tbs salt in it) ~blanch{30%sec}, stems held in for fifteen seconds before the leaves go under, then lift it straight into ice water.

Squeeze @&(~1)spinach{} in a bundle, working from the root end, until no more water comes out of it, then cut it across into 1 1/2-inch lengths so it stands up in the bowl.

Steep @&(~1)squeezed spinach{} in @dashi{1%cup}(240 mL), @soy sauce{2%Tbs}(30 mL) and @mirin{2%Tbs}(30 mL) ~steep{30%min} in the fridge, turning it over once.

Lift @&(~1)ohitashi{} out with chopsticks, squeeze it once lightly so it is damp and not dripping, and top it with @katsuobushi{1%pinch}(a small handful, torn loose).
